Notes on usage and input file format:
- The geometry file should contain two numeric columns: x y, ordered around the body (clockwise or counterclockwise). If the last point does not repeat the first, the script will auto-close by appending the first point.
- Ensure the polygon does not self-intersect and has at least two valid panels after removing duplicates.
- Degenerate panels (zero length) are removed with a warning.

Analytical references implemented:
- Cp on a circle in uniform flow at AoA α: Cp = 1 - 4 sin^2(θ - α).
- Cl for a circle at small α (radians): Cl = 2π α.

Important modeling note:
- A source-only panel method enforces no-penetration but cannot produce lift on a symmetric closed body unless a net circulation is present. Here, circulation is estimated by integrating tangential velocity; for a circle in pure source+uniform flow without a vortex, the theoretical Cl is zero. The small-angle formula Cl = 2π α pertains to lifting flows with Kutta condition (e.g., Joukowski airfoil) or a superimposed vortex on a circle. The code reports both values for transparency; for a circle without a vortex, the computed Cl will be near zero, while the analytical 2π α is the classical lifting-theory reference. The console output clarifies this context via the comparison numbers.

Below is a self-contained MATLAB script that implements a 2-D constant-strength source panel method with a vortex (to enforce the Kutta condition) for a closed airfoil-like body. It:

- Reads panel points from a user-specified text file (CSV or whitespace-delimited, columns x,y).
- Prompts for U∞, α (deg), and ρ.
- Assembles and solves the source–vortex panel system with Kutta condition.
- Computes Cp distribution and lift coefficient (via Kutta–Joukowski).
- Compares Cp and Cl against analytical references:
  - Thin-airfoil theory: Cl ≈ 2π α (α in radians) for small α.
  - Optional circle reference: if geometry is a circle (detected within tolerance), compares Cp to analytical solution for uniform flow with circulation set by Kutta–Joukowski on that circle.
- Includes basic error handling, resampling to vary panel count, and prints accuracy versus panel count.
- Clear comments and simple plotting.

Save as source_panel_method.m and run. It will prompt for the file and parameters.

Code:

function source_panel_method()
    % 2-D Source + Vortex Panel Method with Kutta condition
    % - Reads geometry points (x,y) from a text/CSV file
    % - Prompts for Uinf, alpha (deg), rho
    % - Builds constant-strength source panels + single constant vortex
    % - Enforces no-penetration + Kutta condition -> solve for sigma_j and gamma
    % - Computes Cp and Cl (Kutta–Joukowski)
    % - Compares to thin-airfoil theory; if geometry is close to a circle, also compares Cp to analytical
    % - Option to resample panels and report accuracy vs panel count
    %
    % Author: (Your Name)
    % Date: (Today)

    clc; close all;

    fprintf('2-D Source + Vortex Panel Method with Kutta condition\n');

    %---------------------------
    % Read geometry
    %---------------------------
    [x_in, y_in, path] = readGeometryFile();
    if isempty(x_in)
        fprintf('Aborting due to geometry read error.\n');
        return;
    end

    % Ensure closed polygon (first point = last point)
    [x_in, y_in] = ensureClosed(x_in, y_in);

    %---------------------------
    % Get flow parameters
    %---------------------------
    [Uinf, alpha_deg, rho] = getFlowParams();
    alpha = deg2rad(alpha_deg);

    %---------------------------
    % Ask about panel-count study
    %---------------------------
    doStudy = askYesNo('Perform panel-count accuracy study? (y/n): ');
    if doStudy
        defaultNs = [50 100 200 400];
        Ns = input(sprintf('Enter vector of panel counts (default %s): ', mat2str(defaultNs)));
        if isempty(Ns)
            Ns = defaultNs;
        end
        Ns = unique(max(6, round(Ns(:)'))); % at least 6 panels and integer
    else
        Ns = 0; % sentinel to use original discretization only
    end

    %---------------------------
    % Discretize baseline geometry (original panelization)
    %---------------------------
    [panels0, ok] = buildPanels(x_in, y_in);
    if ~ok
        fprintf('Panel building failed (degenerate or insufficient points).\n');
        return;
    end

    if Ns(1) == 0
        % Only use original discretization
        Ns = numel(panels0.xc); % number of panels inferred from original data
        panelSets = {panels0};
        labels = {sprintf('N=%d (original)', Ns)};
    else
        % Resample geometry arclength-wise to each requested N
        panelSets = cell(1, numel(Ns));
        labels = cell(1, numel(Ns));
        for k = 1:numel(Ns)
            [xr, yr] = resampleClosedCurve(x_in, y_in, Ns(k)+1); % Ns panels => Ns+1 points closed
            [panelsK, ok] = buildPanels(xr, yr);
            if ~ok
                fprintf('Resampling/buildPanels failed for N=%d; skipping.\n', Ns(k));
                panelSets{k} = [];
                labels{k} = sprintf('N=%d (failed)', Ns(k));
            else
                panelSets{k} = panelsK;
                labels{k} = sprintf('N=%d', Ns(k));
            end
        end
    end

    % Detect if geometry is close to a circle (for Cp analytic reference)
    isCircle = detectCircle(x_in, y_in);
    if isCircle
        fprintf('Geometry appears close to a circle. Will include circle Cp reference.\n');
    end

    %---------------------------
    % Solve, compute Cp and Cl for each panelization
    %---------------------------
    results = struct('Cp',[],'Cl',[],'Gamma',[],'xc',[],'yc',[],'theta',[],'s',[],'N',[]);
    resList = [];
    for k = 1:numel(panelSets)
        P = panelSets{k};
        if isempty(P), continue; end

        try
            [Cp, Cl, Gamma] = solveSourceVortexPanels(P, Uinf, alpha, rho);
        catch ME
            fprintf('Solve failed for %s: %s\n', labels{k}, ME.message);
            continue;
        end

        r.Cp = Cp;
        r.Cl = Cl;
        r.Gamma = Gamma;
        r.xc = P.xc;
        r.yc = P.yc;
        r.theta = P.theta;
        r.s = P.s;
        r.N = numel(P.xc);
        resList = [resList; r]; %#ok<AGROW>

        fprintf('%s: Cl = %.6f, Gamma = %.6f\n', labels{k}, Cl, Gamma);
    end

    if isempty(resList)
        fprintf('No successful solutions. Exiting.\n');
        return;
    end

    %---------------------------
    % Analytical references
    %---------------------------
    % Thin-airfoil theory: Cl_ref = 2*pi*alpha (alpha in radians)
    Cl_thin = 2*pi*alpha;
    fprintf('Thin-airfoil theory (small alpha) reference: Cl_ref = %.6f\n', Cl_thin);

    % For circle Cp, if applicable:
    Cp_circle_refs = cell(1, numel(resList));
    if isCircle
        % Estimate circle center and radius
        [xc0, yc0, R] = fitCircle(x_in, y_in);
        for k = 1:numel(resList)
            % Analytical Cp on circle in uniform flow with circulation Gamma
            % Cp(theta) = 1 - (2*Uinf*sin(theta) + Gamma/(2*pi*R))^2 / Uinf^2
            % At panel control points, compute polar angle theta_p wrt circle center
            th = atan2(resList(k).yc - yc0, resList(k).xc - xc0);
            Cp_ref = 1 - ( (2*sin(th) + resList(k).Gamma/(2*pi*R*Uinf)).^2 );
            Cp_circle_refs{k} = Cp_ref;
        end
    end

    %---------------------------
    % Plot Cp distributions
    %---------------------------
    figure('Name','Pressure coefficient Cp'); hold on; grid on;
    set(gca,'YDir','reverse'); % conventional airfoil Cp plot inverted
    colors = lines(numel(resList));
    for k = 1:numel(resList)
        sOverC = resList(k).s / resList(k).s(end); % nondimensional arc length
        plot(sOverC, resList(k).Cp, '-o', 'Color', colors(k,:), 'DisplayName', sprintf('Computed %d panels', resList(k).N));
        if isCircle && ~isempty(Cp_circle_refs{k})
            plot(sOverC, Cp_circle_refs{k}, '--', 'Color', colors(k,:), 'DisplayName', sprintf('Circle ref %d', resList(k).N));
        end
    end
    xlabel('Arc length / chord (approx)');
    ylabel('Cp');
    legend('Location','best');
    title(sprintf('Cp distribution at alpha = %.2f deg', alpha_deg));

    %---------------------------
    % Print lift coefficient comparison vs thin-airfoil theory
    %---------------------------
    fprintf('\nLift coefficient comparison:\n');
    for k = 1:numel(resList)
        err = resList(k).Cl - Cl_thin;
        fprintf('N=%d: Cl=%.6f, thin-airfoil ref=%.6f, error=%.3e\n', resList(k).N, resList(k).Cl, Cl_thin, err);
    end

    %---------------------------
    % If a study was requested, summarize error vs N
    %---------------------------
    if numel(resList) > 1
        Ns_used = arrayfun(@(r) r.N, resList);
        Cls = arrayfun(@(r) r.Cl, resList);
        figure('Name','Cl convergence'); grid on; hold on;
        plot(Ns_used, Cls, '-o', 'DisplayName','Computed Cl');
        yline(Cl_thin, '--', 'DisplayName','Thin-airfoil ref');
        xlabel('Number of panels N');
        ylabel('Cl');
        legend('Location','best');
        title('Lift coefficient vs panel count');
    end

    fprintf('\nDone.\n');
end

function [x, y, path] = readGeometryFile()
    x = []; y = []; path = '';
    try
        [file, pth] = uigetfile({'*.txt;*.dat;*.csv','Text/CSV Files (*.txt,*.dat,*.csv)'; '*.*','All Files'}, 'Select geometry file (x,y columns)');
        if isequal(file,0)
            fprintf('File selection canceled.\n');
            return;
        end
        path = fullfile(pth, file);
        raw = fileread(path);
        if isempty(raw)
            error('Empty file.');
        end
        % Replace commas with spaces, collapse multiple spaces
        raw = regexprep(raw, ',', ' ');
        raw = regexprep(raw, '[;\t]', ' ');
        lines = regexp(raw, '\r\n|\n|\r', 'split');
        data = [];
        for i=1:numel(lines)
            ln = strtrim(lines{i});
            if isempty(ln), continue; end
            if startsWith(ln,'#') || startsWith(ln,'%'), continue; end
            nums = sscanf(ln, '%f');
            if numel(nums) >= 2
                data = [data; nums(1:2)']; %#ok<AGROW>
            end
        end
        if size(data,1) < 3
            error('Need at least 3 points.');
        end
        x = data(:,1);
        y = data(:,2);
    catch ME
        fprintf('Error reading geometry: %s\n', ME.message);
        x = []; y = []; path = '';
    end
end

function [Uinf, alpha_deg, rho] = getFlowParams()
    Uinf = inputWithDefault('Free-stream speed Uinf [m/s] (default 1): ', 1.0, @(v) isscalar(v) && v>0);
    alpha_deg = inputWithDefault('Angle of attack alpha [deg] (default 5): ', 5.0, @(v) isscalar(v));
    rho = inputWithDefault('Fluid density rho [kg/m^3] (default 1.225): ', 1.225, @(v) isscalar(v) && v>0);
end

function val = inputWithDefault(prompt, defaultVal, validator)
    if nargin < 3, validator = @(v) true; end
    s = input(prompt, 's');
    if isempty(s)
        val = defaultVal;
        return;
    end
    v = str2double(s);
    if ~isfinite(v) || ~validator(v)
        fprintf('Invalid input. Using default %g.\n', defaultVal);
        val = defaultVal;
    else
        val = v;
    end
end

function yn = askYesNo(prompt)
    s = lower(strtrim(input(prompt, 's')));
    yn = ~isempty(s) && any(s(1) == 'y');
end

function [x, y] = ensureClosed(x, y)
    if x(1) ~= x(end) || y(1) ~= y(end)
        x = [x; x(1)];
        y = [y; y(1)];
    end
end

function [panels, ok] = buildPanels(x, y)
    % Build constant-strength straight panels from a closed polygon
    ok = false;
    npt = numel(x);
    if npt < 3, panels = []; return; end
    % Remove duplicated interior consecutive points
    mask = [true; hypot(diff(x), diff(y)) > 1e-12];
    x = x(mask); y = y(mask);
    if x(1) ~= x(end) || y(1) ~= y(end)
        x = [x; x(1)];
        y = [y; y(1)];
    end
    N = numel(x)-1;
    if N < 3, panels = []; return; end

    % Panel end points
    x1 = x(1:N); y1 = y(1:N);
    x2 = x(2:N+1); y2 = y(2:N+1);

    % Panel geometry
    dx = x2 - x1; dy = y2 - y1;
    L = hypot(dx, dy);
    if any(L < 1e-14)
        panels = []; return;
    end
    theta = atan2(dy, dx); % panel orientation angle
    xc = 0.5*(x1 + x2);
    yc = 0.5*(y1 + y2);

    % Panel local unit vectors
    tx = cos(theta); ty = sin(theta);        % tangential (pointing from 1->2)
    nx = -sin(theta); ny = cos(theta);       % normal (90 deg CCW from tangent)

    % Ensure normals point outward: determine polygon orientation (CCW = area > 0)
    area2 = 0.5*sum(x1.*y2 - x2.*y1);
    ccw = area2 > 0;
    % For CCW ordered points, outward normal is +n as defined. For CW, flip normals.
    if ~ccw
        nx = -nx; ny = -ny;
    end

    % Accumulated arclength for plotting
    s = [0; cumsum(L(:))];

    panels = struct('x1',x1,'y1',y1,'x2',x2,'y2',y2,'L',L,'theta',theta,...
                    'xc',xc,'yc',yc,'tx',tx,'ty',ty,'nx',nx,'ny',ny,'s',s);
    ok = true;
end

function [Cp, Cl, Gamma] = solveSourceVortexPanels(P, Uinf, alpha, rho)
    % Build influence matrices for constant source strengths sigma_j and a single constant vortex gamma
    % Unknowns: [sigma_1..sigma_N, gamma]^T
    % Equations:
    % - No-penetration at each control point i: (U · n_i) + sum_j N^sigma_ij * sigma_j + N^gamma_i * gamma = 0
    % - Kutta condition: tangential velocity equal at trailing edge from upper/lower (here implemented as sum of tangential due to sources at TE control points equal; gamma enforces proper finite trailing-edge velocity)
    % We use a standard linear Kutta condition: sum_j (T influence at TE_upper) * sigma_j + gamma/2 + U·t_upper = sum_j (T influence at TE_lower) * sigma_j - gamma/2 + U·t_lower
    % This leads to an equation linear in [sigma, gamma].
    %
    % Note: Requires identifying a trailing edge as the point of maximum x (or a cusp). Here we choose the panel with maximum x among endpoints as TE and split into "upper/lower" adjacent panels.

    N = numel(P.xc);
    % Free-stream components
    Ux = Uinf*cos(alpha);
    Uy = Uinf*sin(alpha);

    % Build influence matrices for sources and the single vortex in normal and tangential direction
    % For each control point i and panel j, compute:
    % N^sigma_ij = normal velocity at i due to unit source on panel j
    % T^sigma_ij = tangential velocity at i due to unit source on panel j
    % N^gamma_i = normal velocity at i due to unit-strength vortex (constant gamma on all panels -> equivalent to line vortex distributed? Common implementation: add a constant-strength vortex on each panel with same gamma; total tangential influence accounted)
    % Here we use standard closed-body source-vortex formulation (Katz & Plotkin): add column for a unit vortex on each panel with identical strength gamma; the induced velocities are sum over panels of vortex influence with strength gamma. This column is computed as the tangential contribution of a unit vortex panel summed along the body; for normals we compute their normal components.

    % Preallocate
    A = zeros(N+1, N+1);
    rhs = zeros(N+1, 1);

    % Influence matrices (normal)
    Nsig = zeros(N,N);
    Ngam = zeros(N,1);
    % For Kutta row, we need tangential influences
    Tsig = zeros(1,N);
    Tgam = 0;
    % Also need tangential at two TE-adjacent control points
    % Identify trailing edge panels: pick vertex with max x, find adjacent panels
    [iTE, te_is_end1] = findTrailingEdge(P);
    % Upper/lower control point indices adjacent to TE
    % We'll define iU = iTE (panel ending/starting at TE) and iL = mod(iTE,N)+1 (next panel)
    iU = iTE;
    iL = wrap(iTE+1, N);

    % Build source and vortex influences
    for i = 1:N
        xi = P.xc(i); yi = P.yc(i);
        nxi = P.nx(i); nyi = P.ny(i);
        txi = P.tx(i); tyi = P.ty(i);
        for j = 1:N
            % Transform control point i into local coordinates of panel j
            [I_n, I_t] = sourceInfluenceOnPoint(xi, yi, j, P);
            Nsig(i,j) = I_n;
            if i == iU
                Tsig(1,j) = Tsig(1,j) + I_t;
            elseif i == iL
                % accumulate on iL row if needed later
            end
        end
        % Vortex influence column: unit vortex on each panel with same strength gamma
        % For a unit vortex distributed on panel j (constant over panel), induced velocity at point i has normal and tangential components.
        % Compute and sum over all panels.
        [In_gam, It_gam] = vortexColumnInfluence(xi, yi, P);
        Ngam(i,1) = In_gam;
        if i == iU
            Tgam = Tgam + It_gam;
        end
    end

    % Assemble no-penetration rows
    for i = 1:N
        A(i,1:N) = Nsig(i,1:N);
        A(i,N+1) = Ngam(i,1);
        rhs(i) = - (Ux*P.nx(i) + Uy*P.ny(i));
    end

    % Build Kutta condition row using tangential velocities at TE-adjacent control points
    % Compute tangential sums for iU and iL
    Tsig_iU = Tsig; % already filled during loop when i==iU
    % For iL, we need to fill similarly
    Tsig_iL = zeros(1,N);
    for j=1:N
        [~, I_t_L] = sourceInfluenceOnPoint(P.xc(iL), P.yc(iL), j, P);
        Tsig_iL(1,j) = I_t_L;
    end
    % Vortex column tangential at iL
    [~, It_gam_L] = vortexColumnInfluence(P.xc(iL), P.yc(iL), P);

    % Kutta: V_t(iU) = V_t(iL)
    % (U·t_iU) + sum_j Tsig_iU*sigma_j + Tgam*gamma = (U·t_iL) + sum_j Tsig_iL*sigma_j + It_gam_L*gamma
    A(N+1,1:N) = Tsig_iU - Tsig_iL;
    A(N+1,N+1) = Tgam - It_gam_L;
    rhs(N+1) = (Ux*P.tx(iL) + Uy*P.ty(iL)) - (Ux*P.tx(iU) + Uy*P.ty(iU));

    % Solve linear system
    sol = A\rhs;
    sigma = sol(1:N);
    gamma = sol(N+1);

    % Compute tangential velocity on each panel control point
    Ut = zeros(N,1);
    for i = 1:N
        Ut(i) = Ux*P.tx(i) + Uy*P.ty(i);
        for j = 1:N
            [~, It] = sourceInfluenceOnPoint(P.xc(i), P.yc(i), j, P);
            Ut(i) = Ut(i) + It*sigma(j);
        end
        [~, Itgam] = vortexColumnInfluence(P.xc(i), P.yc(i), P);
        Ut(i) = Ut(i) + Itgam*gamma;
    end

    % Cp = 1 - (|V|/Uinf)^2; since body-fitted, approximate |V| ≈ |V_t|
    Cp = 1 - (Ut./Uinf).^2;

    % Compute total circulation Gamma via gamma variable times panel lengths sum of 1 (since gamma is uniform per panel influence)
    % In this formulation, gamma is the strength of the additional "uniform vortex sheet" along the body: total circulation ~ gamma * sum(L_j) influence scaling is embedded.
    % For lift, more robust is Kutta–Joukowski using panel tangential jump across body; however with this simple model, treat Gamma_effective = gamma * total_length_eff?
    % Alternative: compute circulation numerically by integrating tangential velocity around body minus freestream component along tangent:
    Gamma_num = 0.0;
    for i=1:N
        % Circulation integral ∮ (V · t) ds ≈ sum_i (V_t(i) - U·t_i) * L_i
        Gamma_num = Gamma_num + (Ut(i) - (Ux*P.tx(i) + Uy*P.ty(i))) * P.L(i);
    end
    % Lift per unit span L' = rho * Uinf * Gamma, Cl = L' / (0.5*rho*Uinf^2*c)
    % Estimate chord c as max(x) - min(x)
    chord = max([P.x1; P.x2]) - min([P.x1; P.x2]);
    if chord <= 0
        chord = P.s(end); % fallback to perimeter
    end
    Lprime = rho * Uinf * Gamma_num;
    Cl = Lprime / (0.5*rho*Uinf^2*chord);

    Gamma = Gamma_num; % report numerical circulation
end

function [I_n, I_t] = sourceInfluenceOnPoint(xp, yp, j, P)
    % Influence at point (xp,yp) from unit-strength constant source distributed along panel j
    % Returns:
    % I_n = normal velocity component at (xp,yp) (dot with panel i normal is applied outside)
    % I_t = tangential velocity component at (xp,yp) along the local tangent at the FIELD point
    % Implementation: transform to local coordinates of panel j, use standard log/atan expressions, then rotate to global, then project onto local tangent/normal at FIELD point.
    %
    % References: Katz & Plotkin, Hess & Smith.
    x1 = P.x1(j); y1 = P.y1(j);
    x2 = P.x2(j); y2 = P.y2(j);
    dx = x2 - x1; dy = y2 - y1;
    L = P.L(j);
    cosT = dx / L; sinT = dy / L;

    % Transform field point to panel-local frame (origin at (x1,y1), x along panel)
    xloc =  (xp - x1)*cosT + (yp - y1)*sinT;
    yloc = -(xp - x1)*sinT + (yp - y1)*cosT;

    % Avoid singularities by small epsilon
    epsy = 1e-14;
    yloc = sign(yloc).*max(abs(yloc), epsy);

    % Influence of unit source panel (strength per unit length = 1) in its own local frame:
    % u_x (local) = (1/2π) * ln(R2/R1)
    % u_y (local) = (1/2π) * (atan2((L - xloc), yloc) + atan2(xloc, yloc))
    % where R1 = sqrt(xloc^2 + yloc^2), R2 = sqrt((xloc-L)^2 + yloc^2)
    R1 = hypot(xloc, yloc);
    R2 = hypot(xloc - L, yloc);
    uxl = (1/(2*pi)) * log(R2./R1);
    uyl = (1/(2*pi)) * (atan2(yloc, xloc - L) - atan2(yloc, xloc));

    % Rotate to global frame
    ugx =  uxl*cosT - uyl*sinT;
    ugy =  uxl*sinT + uyl*cosT;

    % Now project to normal and tangential directions at the field point.
    % We need the local tangent/normal at the field point. Approximate with nearest panel orientation?
    % Use global field-point tangent/normal approximated as from the closest panel to (xp,yp) would be complicated.
    % Instead, return components in global, and caller will dot with i-panel normals/tangents.
    % To enable that, this function should return the velocity vector; but to keep API, we return projections along the field-point panel i's tangent/normal at call site.
    % Here, we don't know i; so we return the raw vector via persistent and let caller project. But existing caller expects I_n and I_t scalars...
    % Workaround: we compute I_n and I_t for the CURRENT i at call site. We need field-point directions.
    % So we pass in via persistent the field-point tangent/normal. To keep code simple, compute them here:
    % We don't know field-point tangent/normal; caller must compute dot products itself.
    % Therefore change: return I_n = ug·n_i and I_t = ug·t_i. To do that, we need n_i, t_i at the field point; we can fetch from caller using global variables.
    %
    % Simpler: compute components in the coordinate frame of the field point's panel i set externally.
    %
    % Implementation change: stash the current field vectors in persistent variables set by a setter prior to calls.

    [ti, ni] = getFieldDirections(xp, yp, P);
    I_t = ugx*ti(1) + ugy*ti(2);
    I_n = ugx*ni(1) + ugy*ni(2);
end

function [In_gam, It_gam] = vortexColumnInfluence(xp, yp, P)
    % Influence at point (xp,yp) from a unit-strength "uniform vortex sheet" column:
    % Sum of influences from unit-strength constant vortex on each panel.
    % For a unit vortex panel (constant gamma per unit length = 1), local induced velocity is:
    % u_x(local) = 0
    % u_y(local) = (1/2π) * ln(R2/R1)  with sign conventions swapped vs source
    % See standard formulations: a vortex panel is orthogonal to source panel influences.
    N = numel(P.xc);
    ugx = 0; ugy = 0;
    for j = 1:N
        x1 = P.x1(j); y1 = P.y1(j);
        x2 = P.x2(j); y2 = P.y2(j);
        dx = x2 - x1; dy = y2 - y1;
        L = P.L(j);
        cosT = dx / L; sinT = dy / L;

        xloc =  (xp - x1)*cosT + (yp - y1)*sinT;
        yloc = -(xp - x1)*sinT + (yp - y1)*cosT;
        epsy = 1e-14;
        yloc = sign(yloc).*max(abs(yloc), epsy);

        % Vortex panel unit-strength influence in local frame:
        uxl = (1/(2*pi)) * (atan2(yloc, xloc - L) - atan2(yloc, xloc));
        uyl = -(1/(2*pi)) * log(hypot(xloc - L, yloc)./hypot(xloc, yloc));

        % Rotate to global
        ugx = ugx + (uxl*cosT - uyl*sinT);
        ugy = ugy + (uxl*sinT + uyl*cosT);
    end

    [ti, ni] = getFieldDirections(xp, yp, P);
    It_gam = ugx*ti(1) + ugy*ti(2);
    In_gam = ugx*ni(1) + ugy*ni(2);
end

function [ti, ni] = getFieldDirections(xp, yp, P)
    % Approximate field-point tangent/normal by using the nearest panel center
    d2 = (P.xc - xp).^2 + (P.yc - yp).^2;
    [~, idx] = min(d2);
    ti = [P.tx(idx); P.ty(idx)];
    ni = [P.nx(idx); P.ny(idx)];
end

function [iTE, te_is_end1] = findTrailingEdge(P)
    % Heuristic: pick vertex with maximum x as TE.
    % Find which panel(s) share that vertex.
    [~, idx1] = max([P.x1; P.x2]);
    if idx1 <= numel(P.x1)
        iTE = idx1;
        te_is_end1 = true;
    else
        iTE = idx1 - numel(P.x1);
        te_is_end1 = false;
    end
    % Bound iTE
    N = numel(P.xc);
    iTE = max(1, min(N, iTE));
end

function j = wrap(i, N)
    j = mod(i-1, N) + 1;
end

function [xr, yr] = resampleClosedCurve(x, y, M)
    % Resample closed curve given by points x,y (assumed first=last) to M points (first=last)
    [x, y] = ensureClosed(x, y);
    s = [0; cumsum(hypot(diff(x), diff(y)))];
    L = s(end);
    si = linspace(0, L, M).';
    % Interpolate with periodic linear interpolation
    xr = interp1(s, x, si, 'linear');
    yr = interp1(s, y, si, 'linear');
    % Fix potential NaN at end due to floating precision
    xr(end) = xr(1); yr(end) = yr(1);
end

function isC = detectCircle(x, y)
    try
        [xc, yc, R] = fitCircle(x, y);
        r = hypot(x - xc, y - yc);
        relErr = std(r)/R;
        isC = isfinite(R) && R>0 && relErr < 0.02; % within 2% radial std dev
    catch
        isC = false;
    end
end

function [xc, yc, R] = fitCircle(x, y)
    % Simple least squares circle fit (Kasa)
    x = x(:); y = y(:);
    A = [2*x, 2*y, ones(size(x))];
    b = x.^2 + y.^2;
    sol = A\b;
    xc = sol(1); yc = sol(2);
    c = sol(3);
    R = sqrt(xc^2 + yc^2 + c);
end

Notes and usage tips:
- Input file should contain at least 3 points and ideally define a closed loop. If not closed, the script closes it automatically by appending the first point at the end.
- The Kutta condition heuristic may not be robust for all airfoils; for NACA-like geometries, picking the max-x point as the trailing edge usually works. You can modify findTrailingEdge to select a known TE index.
- The lift coefficient uses a numerical circulation integral around the body, which is robust and independent of the particular gamma-column normalization.
- The Cp computation uses the tangential component at control points; for small thickness airfoils, this is standard in panel methods.
- The “circle” Cp reference is provided only when the input geometry is detected to be close to a circle. For general airfoils, thin-airfoil Cl is the primary reference.
- The panel-count study resamples the geometry uniformly in arclength. For airfoils with sharp TEs, clustering near LE/TE improves accuracy but is not implemented here for simplicity.

Below is a complete MATLAB program organized as requested. Save each function in its own .m file in the same folder. The main entry point is run_panel_method.m. The program reads boundary points, performs constant-strength source panel analysis for multiple panel counts, computes Cp and Cl, compares to references (circle and NACA thin airfoil theory), performs convergence analysis, and saves Cp distributions to CSV. No external toolboxes are used.

