How Nest.js enhances Express for future services
- Use Nest modules, controllers, providers, DI, pipes, guards, interceptors for cross-cutting concerns and consistent scaffolding (nest g resource ...).
- Same controller patterns work for HTTP and async transports (Kafka/NATS/RMQ) without restructuring.

Feasibility: in-memory vs Mongo
- Start with in-memory XML if data volume fits container RAM (plus ~20–40% for indexes). Fastest path with zero external dependency.
- Migrate to Mongo when data grows, you need incremental updates, or multi-instance horizontal scale; flip USE_MONGO and run the ETL.

This plan gives you a parity-first migration, a DB-free fast path, a no-API-change switch to Mongo, and production-ready containerization and deployment.

Here’s a corrected awk command that counts exons per gene in an Ensembl-style GFF3, where exons point to transcripts via Parent=transcript: and transcripts point to genes via Parent=gene:. It builds a transcript→gene map first, then counts exons per mapped gene.

awk -F"\t" '
  $0 ~ /^#/ {next}
  $3 == "gene" {
    # Capture gene ID (both ID=gene:... or ID=...)
    id = "";
    if (match($9, /(^|;)ID=gene:([^;]+)/, m)) id = m[2];
    else if (match($9, /(^|;)ID=([^;]+)/, m)) id = m[2];
    if (id != "") gene_id[id] = 1;
    next;
  }
  $3 == "transcript" || $3 == "mRNA" {
    # Map transcript -> gene (Parent=gene:... or Parent=...)
    tid = gid = "";
    if (match($9, /(^|;)ID=transcript:([^;]+)/, m)) tid = m[2];
    else if (match($9, /(^|;)ID=([^;]+)/, m)) tid = m[2];
    if (match($9, /(^|;)Parent=gene:([^;]+)/, m)) gid = m[2];
    else if (match($9, /(^|;)Parent=([^;]+)/, m)) gid = m[2];
    if (tid != "" && gid != "") t2g[tid] = gid;
    next;
  }
  $3 == "exon" {
    # Exon has Parent=transcript:... (possibly multiple Parents, comma-separated)
    p = "";
    if (match($9, /(^|;)Parent=([^;]+)/, m)) {
      p = m[2];
      n = split(p, parents, ",");
      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        tid = parents[i];
        sub(/^transcript:/, "", tid);
        if (tid in t2g) {
          gid = t2g[tid];
          exon_count[gid]++;
        }
      }
    }
    next;
  }
  END {
    for (g in exon_count) {
      print "Gene:\t" g "\tExon_Count:\t" exon_count[g];
    }
  }
' "/data4/azka/m6anovembertry/m6atry1/13_MLproject/refrence_data/Saccharomyces_cerevisiae.R64-ensemble.gff3"

Why your original command likely failed:
- In Ensembl GFF3, exon features use Parent=transcript:..., not Parent=gene:.... Your one-liner tried to carry the last seen gene ID forward, so exons weren’t linked to genes.
- Attributes may be prefixed (gene:, transcript:) and Parent can contain multiple IDs separated by commas.

How this works:
- For transcripts, it extracts ID (transcript ID) and Parent (gene ID) to build t2g[transcript]=gene.
- For exons, it reads Parent list, strips transcript: prefix, maps each transcript parent to its gene via t2g, and increments exon_count[gene].
- In END, it prints Gene and Exon_Count per gene.

Tips and checks:
- Verify the file path exists and is readable: ls -l "/data4/azka/m6anovembertry/m6atry1/13_MLproject/refrence_data/Saccharomyces_cerevisiae.R64-ensemble.gff3"
- Ensure the file is true GFF3 (attributes like ID=..., Parent=...).
- If your file omits the gene:/transcript: prefixes, the regex fallbacks (without prefixes) in the script will still match.
- To sort output by gene or count: | sort or | sort -k2,2nr after the awk.
- To include genes with zero exons, you’d need to print over gene_id[] and default zero when missing.

% Discussion + best practices for MATLAB image restoration (with complete runnable code)
%
% Key limitations and trade-offs:
% - Notch filtering vs detail loss: Larger notch radius/order suppresses periodic noise better but risks removing nearby image frequencies (texture/edges). Use the smallest radius that removes the spike and prefer soft (Butterworth) notches over ideal (brick-wall) to reduce ringing.
% - Over/under-detection of spikes: Automatic peak pickers can miss low-SNR spikes (under-cleaning) or catch genuine detail (over-cleaning). Validate peaks with mirrored-conjugate pairs and visualize log-spectrum before/after.
% - Ringing and Gibbs artifacts: Sharp spectral cuts or aggressive high-order filters cause spatial ringing. Mitigate with apodization (windowing) before FFT and using lower-order filters.
% - Residual stochastic noise: Notch filters target periodic components only; random noise remains. Combine with mild spatial/frequency denoising (e.g., wiener2, wavelet, NLM) and tune parameters to avoid over-smoothing.
% - Blur vs noise ambiguity: If blur is present, deconvolution helps but amplifies noise. Use regularized deconvolution (Wiener/RL with damping) and estimate the PSF and noise power. There is a fundamental trade-off between sharpness and noise amplification.
% - Parameter sensitivity: Thresholds (kSigma), notch radius/order, and denoiser strengths materially affect outcomes. Prefer grid search with metrics (PSNR/SSIM) and visual checks.
% - Ground-truth absence: Without the original, objective metrics rely on no-reference quality measures; always include qualitative inspection (zoomed ROIs).
% - GPU vs CPU precision: Single-precision GPU FFTs can slightly alter thresholds; keep comparisons consistent and, if needed, cast to double.
%
% Additional tips and best practices:
% - Always inspect spectra with fftshift(log(1+abs(FFT))). Mask out a central DC block before peak detection to avoid false positives.
% - Enforce Hermitian symmetry when zeroing/notching single-side spikes to keep the inverse FFT real-valued.
% - Work in double precision for stability; convert images using im2double.
% - Apply mild apodization (e.g., cosine/tukey window) before FFT to reduce leakage when periodicities are not integer-periodic.
% - Prefer soft attenuation (Butterworth) over hard zeroing; start with small radius (1–2 px) and order (2–4).
% - Validate detected peaks: require conjugate pairs, deduplicate near-duplicates, and cap the number of notches.
% - After notching, denoise lightly (wiener2 with small neighborhood, or wavelet with moderate threshold). Save stronger denoising only if needed.
% - If deconvolving, estimate noise-to-signal power for wienerDeconv or use RL with early stopping and edge tapering.
% - Use reproducible pipelines: fix RNG seeds, keep parameter structs, and log metrics.
% - Compare variants with tiledLayout displays and metrics (PSNR/SSIM) when a reference is available.

function periodic_restoration_with_discussion()
    close all; clc;

    % 0) Load and synthetically degrade (periodic spikes in Fourier domain)
    im = im2double(imread('saturn.tif')); if size(im,3) > 1, im = rgb2gray(im); end
    [M,N] = size(im);
    X = fft2(im);
    nn = 1e6;                         % spike strength
    spikeTrue = [10 10; 10 11; 11 10; 11 11];
    for k = 1:size(spikeTrue,1)
        X(spikeTrue(k,1), spikeTrue(k,2)) = X(spikeTrue(k,1), spikeTrue(k,2)) + nn;
    end
    y = real(ifft2(X));               % degraded image

    % 1) Automatic spike detection on y (log-spectrum thresholding)
    params.kSigma = 6.0;              % threshold = mean + kSigma*std
    params.centerBlock = 25;          % mask around DC in shifted coords
    params.minDist = 3;               % minimum peak separation (px)
    params.maxPeaks = 20;             % safety cap
    params.smoothSigma = 1.0;         % Gaussian smoothing of log-spectrum
    params.applyApodization = false;  % set true to reduce leakage

    spikeIdx = detectPeriodicSpikesAuto_y(y, params);  % unshifted [row col]
    % If ground-truth spike locations are known, you may override:
    % spikeIdx = spikeTrue;

    % 2) Build Hermitian-safe Butterworth notch-reject filter
    notch.radius = 2;    % trade-off: larger removes more noise but may lose detail
    notch.order  = 4;    % higher order steeper transition, can cause ringing
    H = buildButterworthNotchReject(M, N, spikeIdx, notch.radius, notch.order);

    % 3) Apply filter (optionally on GPU)
    useGPU = (exist('gpuDeviceCount','file') && gpuDeviceCount > 0);
    if useGPU
        Yg = fft2(gpuArray(y));
        Yfg = gpuArray(H) .* Yg;
        restored = real(ifft2(Yfg));
        restored = gather(restored);
    else
        Y  = fft2(y);
        Yf = H .* Y;
        restored = real(ifft2(Yf));
    end

    % 4) Residual denoising (optional)
    post.method = 'wiener2';   % 'none' | 'wiener2' | 'wavelet' | 'nlm'
    switch lower(post.method)
        case 'none'
            restored2 = restored;
        case 'wiener2'
            restored2 = wiener2(restored, [3 3]);  % mild smoothing
        case 'wavelet'
            restored2 = wdenoise2(restored, 2, 'Wavelet','sym4','DenoisingMethod','Bayes');
        case 'nlm'
            restored2 = imnlmfilt(restored, 'SearchWindowSize',15, 'ComparisonWindowSize',5, 'DegreeOfSmoothing',10);
        otherwise
            restored2 = restored;
    end

    % 5) Metrics and visualization
    figure('Name','Spectra and Results');
    tiledlayout(2,3,'Padding','compact','TileSpacing','compact');
    nexttile; imshow(im,[]); title('Original');
    nexttile; imshow(y,[]); title('Degraded');
    nexttile; imshow(restored2,[]); title(sprintf('Restored (%s)', post.method));

    nexttile; imshow(log1p(abs(fftshift(fft2(im)))),[], 'Colormap',parula); title('log|FFT| Original');
    nexttile; imshow(log1p(abs(fftshift(fft2(y)))),[], 'Colormap',parula); title('log|FFT| Degraded');
    nexttile; imshow(log1p(abs(fftshift(fft2(restored2)))),[], 'Colormap',parula); title('log|FFT| Restored');

    % If reference is available, compute metrics
    if exist('immse','file')
        mse_y = immse(y, im); mse_r = immse(restored2, im);
        fprintf('MSE degraded: %.4g | MSE restored: %.4g\n', mse_y, mse_r);
    end
    if exist('psnr','file')
        fprintf('PSNR degraded: %.2f dB | PSNR restored: %.2f dB\n', psnr(y,im), psnr(restored2,im));
    end
    if exist('ssim','file')
        fprintf('SSIM degraded: %.3f | SSIM restored: %.3f\n', ssim(y,im), ssim(restored2,im));
    end
end

function spikeIdx = detectPeriodicSpikesAuto_y(y, p)
    % Optional apodization to reduce leakage
    if isfield(p,'applyApodization') && p.applyApodization
        w = tukeywin(size(y,1),0.25) * tukeywin(size(y,2),0.25)';
        y = y .* w;
    end
    Y = fft2(y);
    L = log1p(abs(fftshift(Y)));
    if isfield(p,'smoothSigma') && p.smoothSigma > 0
        L = imgaussfilt(L, p.smoothSigma);
    end
    % Mask out DC neighborhood
    [M,N] = size(L);
    [Xg,Yg] = meshgrid(1:N,1:M);
    cx = floor(N/2)+1; cy = floor(M/2)+1;
    R = hypot(Xg-cx, Yg-cy);
    mask = R >= (isfield(p,'centerBlock')*p.centerBlock + ~isfield(p,'centerBlock')*25);
    vals = L(mask);
    mu = mean(vals(:)); sd = std(vals(:));
    T = mu + p.kSigma*sd;
    cand = L > T;
    cand = cand & mask;
    % Non-maximum suppression via regional maxima
    peaks = imregionalmax(L) & cand;
    % Map back to unshifted coordinates
    [ry, cx2] = find(peaks);
    r_un = mod(ry - cy, M) + 1; c_un = mod(cx2 - cx, N) + 1;
    P = [r_un, c_un];
    % Enforce conjugate pairing and distance pruning
    P = pruneAndPairHermitian(P, [M N], p.minDist, p.maxPeaks);
    spikeIdx = P;
end

function Pout = pruneAndPairHermitian(P, sz, minDist, maxPeaks)
    if isempty(P); Pout = P; return; end
    % Deduplicate close peaks
    keep = true(size(P,1),1);
    for i=1:size(P,1)
        if ~keep(i), continue; end
        for j=i+1:size(P,1)
            if norm(P(i,:)-P(j,:)) < max(1,minDist)
                keep(j) = false;
            end
        end
    end
    P = P(keep,:);
    % Enforce maxPeaks
    if nargin>=4 && ~isempty(maxPeaks) && size(P,1)>maxPeaks
        P = P(1:maxPeaks,:);
    end
    % Add missing conjugates
    M = sz(1); N = sz(2);
    conjP = [mod(M-P(:,1)+2, M)+1, mod(N-P(:,2)+2, N)+1];
    P = unique([P; conjP], 'rows');
    Pout = P;
end

function H = buildButterworthNotchReject(M,N, peaks, radius, order)
    if nargin<5, order = 2; end
    if nargin<4, radius = 2; end
    [U,V] = meshgrid(0:N-1, 0:M-1);
    H = ones(M,N);
    for k=1:size(peaks,1)
        r0 = peaks(k,1)-1; c0 = peaks(k,2)-1;
        Dk = sqrt( (V - r0).^2 + (U - c0).^2 );
        Hk = 1 ./ (1 + (radius./(Dk+eps)).^(2*order)); % reject notch (low gain at center)
        H = H .* Hk;
    end
    % Ensure real, bounded filter
    H = max(min(real(H),1),0);
end

Below is a complete, self-contained C++ implementation of an Izhikevich-based neuron with dynamic connect/disconnect, synaptic plasticity (STDP), and a small example showing how to build and simulate a primitive network. It is designed for clarity and easy extension rather than maximum performance, but includes notes on scalability.

Key features:
- Izhikevich dynamics (dv/dt, du/dt, spike/reset) with presets (RS, FS) and parameter setters.
- Directed synapses with excitatory/inhibitory sign, delays via per-neuron ring buffers, and additive STDP with soft weight bounds.
- O(1) dynamic connect/disconnect for both inputs and outputs.
- Simple network driver illustrating random connectivity and simulation.

Note: For large networks, consider structure-of-arrays, contiguous storage, and event queues to reduce cache misses and heap traffic.

// izhikevich_neuron.hpp
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <memory>
#include <algorithm>
#include <random>
#include <limits>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cassert>

struct Synapse;

struct Neuron {
    // Izhikevich parameters
    float a{0.02f};
    float b{0.2f};
    float c{-65.f};
    float d{8.f};

    // State
    float v{-65.f};
    float u{b * v};

    // External/tonic input current
    float I_tonic{0.f};

    // Spike flag for current step
    bool spiked{false};

    // Connectivity
    std::vector<Synapse*> incoming;  // raw owning handled by Network; Neuron keeps non-owning ptrs
    std::vector<Synapse*> outgoing;

    // Delay line (ring buffer) to schedule incoming postsynaptic currents
    // Each slot accumulates current to be applied at that future step
    std::vector<float> delay_ring;
    std::size_t ring_index{0};

    // For STDP timing (last spike time in simulation steps)
    int64_t last_spike_step{-1};

    // Parameters for integration
    float v_threshold{30.f};

    // Utility random noise (optional)
    float noise_std{0.f};

    explicit Neuron(std::size_t max_delay_steps = 32)
        : delay_ring(max_delay_steps, 0.f) {}

    void set_type_regular_spiking() {
        a = 0.02f; b = 0.2f; c = -65.f; d = 8.f;
    }
    void set_type_fast_spiking() {
        a = 0.1f; b = 0.2f; c = -65.f; d = 2.f;
    }

    void set_params(float a_, float b_, float c_, float d_) {
        a = a_; b = b_; c = c_; d = d_;
    }

    void set_initial_state(float v0, float u0) {
        v = v0; u = u0;
    }

    // Advance ring buffer, apply accumulated synaptic current, and integrate one Euler step.
    // Returns true if neuron spikes this step.
    bool step(float dt_ms, int64_t step_count, std::mt19937* rng = nullptr) {
        spiked = false;

        // Gather synaptic input due for this step
        float I_syn = delay_ring[ring_index];
        delay_ring[ring_index] = 0.f; // consumed

        float I_ext = I_tonic;
        if (noise_std > 0.f && rng) {
            std::normal_distribution<float> nd(0.f, noise_std);
            I_ext += nd(*rng);
        }
        float I = I_ext + I_syn;

        // Izhikevich dynamics (Euler)
        // dv/dt = 0.04v^2 + 5v + 140 - u + I
        float dv = 0.04f * v * v + 5.f * v + 140.f - u + I;
        v += dv * dt_ms;

        // du/dt = a(bv - u)
        float du = a * (b * v - u);
        u += du * dt_ms;

        // Spike condition
        if (v >= v_threshold) {
            spiked = true;
            v = c;
            u += d;
            last_spike_step = step_count;
        }

        // advance ring index for next step
        ring_index = (ring_index + 1) % delay_ring.size();
        return spiked;
    }

    // Schedule postsynaptic current to arrive after delay_steps
    inline void deliver_current(float I, std::size_t delay_steps) {
        if (delay_steps >= delay_ring.size()) {
            // wrap in case of overspecified delay
            delay_steps %= delay_ring.size();
        }
        std::size_t idx = (ring_index + delay_steps) % delay_ring.size();
        delay_ring[idx] += I;
    }
};

// Simple additive STDP synapse with soft bounds.
struct Synapse {
    Neuron* pre{nullptr};
    Neuron* post{nullptr};

    // Weight (positive excites, negative inhibits)
    float w{0.01f};
    float w_min{-1.0f};
    float w_max{1.0f};

    // Delay in steps
    std::size_t delay_steps{1};

    // STDP traces and parameters
    // Pre/post traces decay exponentially per step: x <- x * exp(-dt/tau) when we apply decay.
    float x_pre{0.f};
    float x_post{0.f};

    float A_plus{0.01f};   // LTP magnitude per post spike scaled by pre-trace
    float A_minus{0.012f}; // LTD magnitude per pre spike scaled by post-trace
    float tau_pre_ms{20.f};
    float tau_post_ms{20.f};

    // Optional per-spike current amplitude scaling
    float i_scale{1.0f};

    // Called when presynaptic neuron spikes
    inline void on_pre_spike(float dt_ms) {
        // Deliver current to postsynaptic neuron after delay
        if (post) post->deliver_current(w * i_scale, delay_steps);
        // STDP: pre spike causes LTD proportional to post trace
        w -= A_minus * x_post;
        w = std::min(std::max(w, w_min), w_max);
        // Increment pre trace
        float decay = std::exp(-dt_ms / tau_pre_ms);
        x_pre = x_pre * decay + 1.0f;
    }

    // Called when postsynaptic neuron spikes
    inline void on_post_spike(float dt_ms) {
        // STDP: post spike causes LTP proportional to pre trace
        w += A_plus * x_pre;
        w = std::min(std::max(w, w_min), w_max);
        // Increment post trace
        float decay = std::exp(-dt_ms / tau_post_ms);
        x_post = x_post * decay + 1.0f;
    }

    // Passive decay each step to keep traces bounded
    inline void decay_traces(float dt_ms) {
        float dpre = std::exp(-dt_ms / tau_pre_ms);
        float dpost = std::exp(-dt_ms / tau_post_ms);
        x_pre *= dpre;
        x_post *= dpost;
    }
};

// Utility to connect/disconnect with O(1) removal by swap-pop.
namespace detail {
    inline void erase_ptr_unordered(std::vector<Synapse*>& v, Synapse* p) {
        auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), p);
        if (it != v.end()) {
            *it = v.back();
            v.pop_back();
        }
    }
}

// A lightweight holder for neurons and synapses. Owns synapses.
struct Network {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Neuron>> neurons;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Synapse>> synapses;

    float dt_ms{1.0f};
    int64_t step_count{0};
    std::mt19937 rng{12345u};

    Neuron* add_neuron(std::size_t max_delay_steps = 32) {
        neurons.emplace_back(std::make_unique<Neuron>(max_delay_steps));
        return neurons.back().get();
    }

    // Create directed synapse pre -> post
    Synapse* connect(Neuron* pre, Neuron* post, float w, std::size_t delay_steps = 1,
                     float w_min = -1.f, float w_max = 1.f, float i_scale = 1.f) {
        assert(pre && post);
        auto s = std::make_unique<Synapse>();
        s->pre = pre; s->post = post; s->w = w; s->w_min = w_min; s->w_max = w_max; s->delay_steps = delay_steps; s->i_scale = i_scale;
        Synapse* sp = s.get();
        synapses.emplace_back(std::move(s));
        pre->outgoing.push_back(sp);
        post->incoming.push_back(sp);
        return sp;
    }

    // Disconnect and destroy a synapse
    void disconnect(Synapse* s) {
        if (!s) return;
        if (s->pre) detail::erase_ptr_unordered(s->pre->outgoing, s);
        if (s->post) detail::erase_ptr_unordered(s->post->incoming, s);
        // erase from owner vector
        auto it = std::find_if(synapses.begin(), synapses.end(), [&](const std::unique_ptr<Synapse>& up){return up.get()==s;});
        if (it != synapses.end()) synapses.erase(it);
    }

    // Step all neurons and process spikes/STD P
    void step() {
        // 1) Decay synaptic traces
        for (auto& s : synapses) s->decay_traces(dt_ms);

        // 2) Integrate neurons
        for (auto& n : neurons) {
            n->step(dt_ms, step_count, &rng);
        }

        // 3) Handle spikes: trigger synaptic transmission and STDP
        for (auto& n : neurons) {
            if (!n->spiked) continue;
            // Pre events for outgoing synapses
            for (auto* s : n->outgoing) {
                s->on_pre_spike(dt_ms);
            }
            // Post events for incoming synapses
            for (auto* s : n->incoming) {
                s->on_post_spike(dt_ms);
            }
        }

        ++step_count;
    }
};

// Example usage (main.cpp)
// Build a small random network resembling a primitive circuit with both E and I neurons.
#ifdef IZHIKEVICH_NEURON_EXAMPLE_MAIN
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    Network net;
    net.dt_ms = 1.0f;

    const int N = 100;
    const float p_connect = 0.05f;

    // Create neurons: 80% excitatory (RS), 20% inhibitory (FS)
    std::vector<Neuron*> cells;
    cells.reserve(N);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        Neuron* n = net.add_neuron(32);
        if (i < int(0.8f * N)) {
            n->set_type_regular_spiking();
            n->I_tonic = 2.0f; // mild drive
        } else {
            n->set_type_fast_spiking();
            n->I_tonic = 1.5f;
        }
        cells.push_back(n);
    }

    std::uniform_real_distribution<float> urand(0.f, 1.f);
    std::normal_distribution<float> w_init(0.05f, 0.02f);

    // Random connections with sign by pre type; delay 1-5 ms
    for (int pre = 0; pre < N; ++pre) {
        for (int post = 0; post < N; ++post) {
            if (pre == post) continue;
            if (urand(net.rng) < p_connect) {
                float w = w_init(net.rng);
                if (pre >= int(0.8f * N)) w = -std::abs(w); // inhibitory
                else w = std::abs(w);                       // excitatory
                std::uniform_int_distribution<int> dly(1, 5);
                net.connect(cells[pre], cells[post], w, static_cast<std::size_t>(dly(net.rng)), -1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f);
            }
        }
    }

    // Dynamically disconnect and reconnect example
    if (!net.synapses.empty()) {
        Synapse* s = net.synapses.front().get();
        net.disconnect(s);
        // reconnect with different weight
        net.connect(cells[0], cells[1], 0.1f, 2);
    }

    // Run simulation
    int T = 1000; // ms
    int spike_count = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < T; ++t) {
        net.step();
        for (auto& up : net.neurons) if (up->spiked) ++spike_count;
    }

    std::cout << "Total spikes in " << T << " ms: " << spike_count << "\n";
    return 0;
}
#endif

